          {
            "role": "user",
            "content": "{\"task\":\"Review the supplied saved script for bridge and settle coverage, coherence, depth, fidelity, voice, causal transitions, repetition and source evidence. Return explicit pass/fail/not_checked findings with reasons. Do not treat missing historic metadata as equivalence.\",\"evidence\":{\"case\":\"ch19-seq102\",\"savedScriptSHA256\":\"d566512e608bedb618ca51ca7038dd56e009648e898031b7045e96070d8915e8\",\"clip\":{\"index\":19,\"id\":\"1_jf6119-0-0\",\"title\":\"Jesus Calms the Storm\"},\"passage\":{\"reference\":\"Luke 8:22-25\",\"osisRef\":\"Luke.8.22-Luke.8.25\"},\"sourceExcerpt\":\"The event in our Lord's life described in these verses is related three times in the Gospels. Matthew, Mark, and Luke — were all inspired to record it. This circumstance should teach us the importance of the event, and should make us \\\"give the more heed\\\" to the lessons it contains.\\n\\nWe see, secondly, in these verses — what fears and anxiety may assault the hearts of true disciples of Christ. We read, that \\\"when a storm of wind came down on the lake,\\\" and the boat in which our Lord was sailing was filled with water, and in jeopardy, His companions were greatly alarmed. \\\"They came to Him and awoke Him, saying: Master, Master, we are going to perish!\\\"\\n\\nThey forgot, for a moment, their Master's never-failing care for them in time past. They forgot that so long as they were with Him — they must be safe, whatever happened. It is only too true that sight, and sense, and feeling — make men very poor theologians.\\n\\nFacts like these are sadly humbling to the pride of human nature. It ought to lower our pride and high thoughts, to see what a poor creature is man, even at his best estate — but facts like these are deeply instructive. They teach us what to watch and pray against in our own hearts. They teach of what we must make up our minds to find in other Christians. We must be moderate in our expectations. We must not suppose that men cannot be believers — if they sometimes exhibit great weakness. We must not think that men have no grace — because they are sometimes overwhelmed with fears. Even Peter, James, and John, could cry, \\\"Master, Master, we are going to perish!\\\"\\n\\nWe see, lastly, in these verses — how needful it is for Christians to keep their faith ready for use. We read that our Lord said to His disciples when the storm had ceased, and their fears had subsided, \\\"Where is your faith?\\\" Well might He ask that question! Where was the profit of believing — if they could not believe in the time of need? Where was the real value of faith — unless they kept it in active exercise? Where was the benefit of trusting — if they were to trust their Master in sunshine only, but not in storms?\\n\\nThe lesson now before us is one of deep practical importance. To have true saving faith is one thing. To have that faith always ready for use, is quite another thing. Many receive Christ as their Savior, and deliberately commit their souls to Him for time and eternity — who yet often find their faith sadly failing when something unexpected happens, and they are suddenly tried.\\n\\nThese things ought not so to be. We ought to pray that we may have a stock of faith ready for use at a moment's notice, and may never be found unprepared. The highest style of Christian is the man who lives like Moses, \\\"Seeing Him who is invisible.\\\" (Hebrews 11:27.) That man will never be greatly shaken by any storm in life. He will see Jesus near him in the darkest hour — and blue sky behind the blackest cloud!\\n\\nListen to AUDIO\",\"canonicalMediaAvailability\":\"not_available_in_historic_fixture\"},\"savedScript\":{\"date\":\"2026-08-21\",\"clip\":{\"index\":19,\"id\":\"1_jf6119-0-0\",\"title\":\"Jesus Calms the Storm\"},\"passage\":{\"reference\":\"Luke 8:22-25\",\"osisRef\":\"Luke.8.22-Luke.8.25\"},\"title\":\"For anyone who knows Jesus but still feels the panic rising\",\"scripture\":{\"reference\":\"Luke 8:24\",\"text\":\"They came to him, and awoke him, saying, “Master, master, we are dying!” He awoke, and rebuked the wind and the raging of the water, and they ceased, and it was calm.\",\"translation\":\"WEB\",\"needsCanonicalSource\":false},\"reflection\":{\"text\":\"Even true disciples can be overwhelmed by fear when the storm is real enough. The disciples had Jesus right there in the boat with them, yet when the water started coming in they were sure they were going to die. They forgot his care for them. They forgot that as long as they were with him they had to be safe, whatever happened. Sight and sense and feeling make us poor theologians. That's humbling, but it's also instructive. It teaches us what to watch for in our own hearts, and what to expect in other believers. We have to be moderate in our expectations. A person can have real faith and still show great weakness sometimes. Even Peter, James, and John cried out 'Master, we're going to perish!' When the storm had passed, Jesus asked them, 'Where is your faith?' That's the question. Having true saving faith is one thing. Having that faith ready for use when something unexpected happens is another. Many people receive Christ as their Savior and commit their souls to him for time and eternity, yet their faith fails them when they're suddenly tried. The faith that carries you through a storm is the faith that sees him who is invisible. It finds Jesus near you in the darkest hour, and blue sky behind the blackest cloud.\",\"source\":\"J.C. Ryle, Expository Thoughts on the Gospels: Luke\",\"attribution\":\"Adapted from a trusted classic · J.C. Ryle\",\"flavor\":\"commentary\",\"sourceExcerpt\":\"The event in our Lord's life described in these verses is related three times in the Gospels. Matthew, Mark, and Luke — were all inspired to record it. This circumstance should teach us the importance of the event, and should make us \\\"give the more heed\\\" to the lessons it contains.\\n\\nWe see, secondly, in these verses — what fears and anxiety may assault the hearts of true disciples of Christ. We read, that \\\"when a storm of wind came down on the lake,\\\" and the boat in which our Lord was sailing was filled with water, and in jeopardy, His companions were greatly alarmed. \\\"They came to Him and awoke Him, saying: Master, Master, we are going to perish!\\\"\\n\\nThey forgot, for a moment, their Master's never-failing care for them in time past. They forgot that so long as they were with Him — they must be safe, whatever happened. It is only too true that sight, and sense, and feeling — make men very poor theologians.\\n\\nFacts like these are sadly humbling to the pride of human nature. It ought to lower our pride and high thoughts, to see what a poor creature is man, even at his best estate — but facts like these are deeply instructive. They teach us what to watch and pray against in our own hearts. They teach of what we must make up our minds to find in other Christians. We must be moderate in our expectations. We must not suppose that men cannot be believers — if they sometimes exhibit great weakness. We must not think that men have no grace — because they are sometimes overwhelmed with fears. Even Peter, James, and John, could cry, \\\"Master, Master, we are going to perish!\\\"\\n\\nWe see, lastly, in these verses — how needful it is for Christians to keep their faith ready for use. We read that our Lord said to His disciples when the storm had ceased, and their fears had subsided, \\\"Where is your faith?\\\" Well might He ask that question! Where was the profit of believing — if they could not believe in the time of need? Where was the real value of faith — unless they kept it in active exercise? Where was the benefit of trusting — if they were to trust their Master in sunshine only, but not in storms?\\n\\nThe lesson now before us is one of deep practical importance. To have true saving faith is one thing. To have that faith always ready for use, is quite another thing. Many receive Christ as their Savior, and deliberately commit their souls to Him for time and eternity — who yet often find their faith sadly failing when something unexpected happens, and they are suddenly tried.\\n\\nThese things ought not so to be. We ought to pray that we may have a stock of faith ready for use at a moment's notice, and may never be found unprepared. The highest style of Christian is the man who lives like Moses, \\\"Seeing Him who is invisible.\\\" (Hebrews 11:27.) That man will never be greatly shaken by any storm in life. He will see Jesus near him in the darkest hour — and blue sky behind the blackest cloud!\\n\\nListen to AUDIO\"},\"reflectionHighlights\":[\"\",\"\",\"\",\"\",\"\",\"\",\"\",\"\",\"\",\"Where is your faith?\",\"\",\"\",\"\",\"sees him who is invisible\",\"\",\"\"],\"conclusion\":\"Even when the storm rages, his presence ensures you're never truly alone.\",\"question\":\"When fear takes over, what would change if you remembered he's still in the boat with you?\",\"prayer\":\"Bring your fear to Jesus right now and ask him to calm what feels out of control.\",\"mood\":\"peace\",\"voice\":\"male-d\",\"sequence\":102}}"
          }
        ],
